messages.body_plain:
Jonah,
Thank you very much for your answer. A few points:

1. About declaring l and b in transformed parameters. In my previous exponential smoothing type models, this worked well - no compiler warnings and the fit was OK. Also, I see this example in the Stan manual (p67)

transformed parameters {

real<lower=0> sigma[T];

sigma[1] <- sigma1;    //<--------- and sigma1 is declared in data section

for (t in 2:T)

sigma[t] <- sqrt(alpha0

+ alpha1 * pow(r[t-1] - mu, 2)

+ beta1 * pow(sigma[t-1], 2));

}

2. Yes, trendError and levelError should be just parameters - thank you!
3. Correct, y is positive, while error can go both mays, so occasionally I get rejections, but this is not a major problem.
4. I put a minimum value on sigma, because in my previous experience with fitting the Student distributions, when I allowed nu to go to 1 and sigma to 0, I was getting numerical problems. Is it really important to have the same bound in declaration and model? Because the model statement is more restrictive.

Thank you again. I think I can proceed now.

Slawek

